-- R17 - Fills: 1R is the card's planned risk; an adverse fill past half the stop distance is VOID; Asian markets on an
-- Asian-hours proxy (both Macro Takeaways digests).
-- Owner approval, 10 Nov 2026 (workflow review, decision 2: fix the fill rule and restate the one gapped card).
-- Proposed 10 Nov 2026 with dash.propose_rule; apply_after = Sun 15 Nov 2026 20:00 SGT unless "oppose R17".
-- Body, title, rationale and revert_if below are byte-identical to the DB row (body md5 fda09cb4ae51005a76e0e2b741348e0c);
-- the evidence is generalised (the DB copy names the card, its levels and results; this repo is public).
-- Code that goes with it: META key "risk" in _shared/parse.ts, oneR() in _shared/marks.ts, used by dash-mark.
-- To put it in force before the Sunday window (one line in the Supabase SQL editor, project diiwqbxyhtgvozhncoef):
--   update dash.rules set status = 'in_force', decided_at = now(), apply_after = now() where rid = 'R17' and status = 'proposed';
select dash.propose_rule($j${"scope": "both", "title": "Fills: 1R is the card's planned risk; an adverse fill that has used more than half the stop distance is VOID; Asian markets on an Asian-hours proxy", "body": "PLANNED-RISK FILLS (owner, 10 Nov 2026). The spec sets 1R = |entry - stop| at the fill, so a gap between the card and its fill redefines the risk unit: a card whose proxy opens just short of its stop passes NO-CHASE with a huge r/r from the fill, gets a 1R of a few ticks, and an ordinary exit through the stop is booked as several R. Size is set from the risk the card planned, so R is too. Applies in both books to every linear card (thematic, tactical, hedge), cash FX forwards under R07 included; R01 convexity cards (premium = 1R) are unchanged. It replaces \"1R = |entry - stop|\" in the spec's CONVENTIONS and in R07 items 4 and 6, and adds one VOID test at the fill. The fill itself (the open of the first hourly bar after the opened time, R14's exit-bar wait), NO-CHASE, stops, targets and exits are unchanged.\n\n1. PLANNED RISK. risk = |REF - stop| when the card is written (cash FX: |fwd ref - stop|, R07 item 3): the distance its r/r, p0 and EV were computed from. Write it once as the LAST key of the card's META line, \"· risk <x>\" (price units, the stop's decimals), and never edit it: a contract roll or re-basing shifts every level by the same amount and leaves it unchanged, and an R07 roll moves the entry by the new points and leaves it unchanged (R07 item 6's \"1R moves with the entry\" no longer applies).\n\n2. VOID ON AN ADVERSE GAP. At the fill, used = direction x (REF - fill) / risk, direction +1 for a long and -1 for a short (cash FX: fwd ref and the forward fill, so the points cancel). The spec already voids a fill at or beyond the stop (used >= 100%). New: a fill with used above 50%, unrounded, is VOID too: status void, never scored, kept as the spec keeps a VOID, with the LOG and CHANGES line \"Pnn VOID: fill <f> had used <nn>% of the stop distance (R17)\". The view did not fail, the entry did: if the lean still passes THE TRADE TEST in that run or a later one it is carded again as a NEW Pnn at a fresh REF, stop and target. Order at the fill: the spec's VOID (at or beyond stop or target), then this test, then NO-CHASE.\n\n3. P&L IN R. 1R = risk for the life of the card: R = direction x (mark - entry) / risk on every mark line, at the exit, in the SCORECARD and in the calibration lines. A fill on the adverse side that passes item 2 therefore loses less than 1R at its stop and makes more than its carded r/r at its target; a fill on the favourable side that passes NO-CHASE can lose a little more than 1R at its stop. Both are what a position sized on the card's risk does. NO-CHASE keeps its own arithmetic (r/r from the fill, on the actual distances).\n\n4. ASIAN MARKETS FILL IN ASIAN HOURS. A card on an Asian market (Japan, Korea, Taiwan, China, Hong Kong, India, ASEAN) takes a proxy that trades in the Asian session and that Yahoo prices hourly, the local index or a local-hours future (^KS11, ^TWII, ^N225, ^HSI, ^NSEI, 000300.SS and the like), so that it fills in the first Asian hour after the email. A US-listed ETF (EWY, EWT, EWJ, FXI, INDA ...) fills about 13 hours later at the US open, after the Asian day has traded: use one only when no Asian-hours proxy returns live hourly bars, and print on the card \"fills at the US open, <hh:mm> SGT\".\n\n5. CARDS FILLED BEFORE THIS RULE keep the 1R they were filled with: no risk key, no restatement, open or closed. A card still pending when this rule comes into force gets its risk key at the fill, and items 2 and 3 apply to it. ONE RESTATEMENT, Fable book, on the first run under this rule: P40 (filled 3 Nov 2026; its fill had used 94% of the stop distance) becomes VOID. In its META set the status to void and result and exit to \"-\"; add one LOG line and one CHANGES line \"P40 restated VOID (R17): fill had used 94% of the stop distance\"; and rebuild the SCORECARD line and the header's closed count without it: every count and sum it sits in by subtraction (closed, losses, cum R, its class, factor, bucket and crowd side, at gate, thin edge, the calibration lines), every average and hit rate from the adjusted counts and sums, and peak and max DD from the closed list in close-date order, which the database keeps: select pid, closed, result_r from dash.trades where model = 'fable' and status like 'closed%' and pid <> 'P40' order by closed, pid (Supabase project diiwqbxyhtgvozhncoef). If the P40 block has already left the book, restate the SCORECARD and write the LOG and CHANGES lines only. A later run that finds P40 void, or the LOG line already written, does nothing.\n\n6. PRINT. The trade block's footer gains \"1R = the card's planned risk; an adverse fill past half the stop distance is void (R17)\". The header's RULES line is at its 600-character cap and is not changed.", "rationale": "Owner approved the Takeaways workflow review on 10 Nov 2026 (decision 2: one R becomes the planned risk on the card, never the distance left after a gap; a fill that has used over half the stop distance is void; P40 restated void). Dividing by what a gap leaves of the stop distance makes the size of a loss depend on the gap and not on the risk taken, and one such card decides the thin-edge review due at 30 closes.", "evidence": "One tactical card filled at a US open after an overnight gap that had already used 94% of its stop distance: the r/r from the fill passed NO-CHASE, 1R shrank to a few ticks, and an ordinary exit through the stop was booked as a loss of several R. That one card decides the sign of the thin-edge tally. One other past fill sat just over the 50% line and is not restated. Three Asian equity cards on US-listed ETFs each waited about 13 hours for a US open. Card names, levels and results are in the database row only.", "revert_if": "Revert item 2 if, over 8 weeks, 3 or more cards it voided would have reached their target before their stop from the fill while none of the voided cards would have lost; revert item 4 if Asian-hours proxies give stale hourly bars or worse fills on 3 or more cards; revert the whole rule if the owner would in fact take a gapped fill at full size.", "supersedes": null, "review_due": "2026-12-22", "source": "owner"}$j$::jsonb);
